From 55689770407dcfdedd670e69f145cbd0f9cf2ce4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Majid Arasskhani <M.Arasskhani@gosilkroad.net>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 15:36:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 08/22] fix(video): runner stops when its lease is taken over;
 poll loop commits before waiting; cancel yields to a finished provider job
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Bug (B-8): _run_video_job reloaded the row every poll to see cancels but
never checked that it still owned the lease. When a lease expired mid-job
and claim_due_video_jobs handed the row to a second worker, both runners
kept polling, both ingested, both billed. Bug (B-37): each poll iteration
did refresh -> write lease -> sleep -> provider poll -> commit, so the
transaction and its pooled connection stayed open for the whole provider
wait (seconds per iteration, minutes per job). Cancel released the budget
hold and marked the job cancelled even when the provider had already
finished and charged for the clip.

Change:
- LeaseLost: raised after refresh when lease_owner != the claim this runner
  started with; the runner rolls back and returns without touching status,
  the reservation or billing (metric video_job_lease_lost).
- The lease bump is committed before sleep/poll; the write-back after the
  poll is its own short transaction.
- cancel_video_job polls the provider once first; state == completed ->
  VideoJobAlreadyCompleted (API answers 409, the runner ingests and bills
  as usual). Otherwise cancel proceeds unchanged.

Tests: tests/test_video_job_lease.py — a takeover during the first poll
leaves the row to the new owner with no usage row from the old runner;
cancel refuses when the provider reports completed and still works when it
does not.

